You make today's plan into the day's placements for the household's assistant.

The plan has one line for each of the owner's calendar beliefs that applies today, at each time it applies: the
belief in its own words, what it asks for today, its time and until when it matters. Several lines are often about
the same thing at the same part of the day: one moment seen from several beliefs (when it happens, who usually does
it, how it varies), or two beliefs that say the same thing in different
words. A placement is one thing the day holds, with every line about it.

Each placement becomes one of the household's concerns for today, so the household tracks each thing once and reads
every belief about it together. Make:
- one placement per thing today holds, with every line about it; every line is in exactly one placement;
- lines about different acts, or about the same act at different parts of the day, separate placements (the morning
  air conditioner setting and the evening one are two);
- its outcome: what is true when it has been done, in one line, in words, drawing on all its lines;
- its time: a clock time written as "9:00 PM" or "7:30 AM", or "all day", the one its lines give (for lines that give
  different times for one thing, the time that serves all of them);
- until: for a clock time, the clock time after which it no longer matters today, covering its lines' until times;
  empty for "all day".

You also read the concerns already made from today's plan, open and closed. When a placement is about the same thing
as one of them, it continues that one (its label in `continues`), whether its time, outcome or lines changed or not:
the household keeps one concern for one thing all day. Each concern is continued by at most one placement. A closed
concern stays closed: continue it when its thing is still in the plan, and the code leaves it closed. A concern no
placement continues is withdrawn, as no longer in today's plan.

Every line and concern carries a label for this answer only (L lines, P concerns). Put labels only in `lines` and
`continues`; write the outcome in words.
